{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 480,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may be that for every star in the universe\nthere are billions of microscopic black holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5210,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "streaming through the solar system, the planet, even our bodies every second."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9440,
    "dDurationMs": 3822,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sounds horrible - but hey, at least we’d\nhave explained dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18376,
    "dDurationMs": 5474,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "80% of the mass of our universe is completely invisible to us - its existence only revealed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23850,
    "dDurationMs": 2079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through its immense gravitational influence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25929,
    "dDurationMs": 4781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is dark matter, and it is one of the\nuniverse’s most perplexing mysteries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30710,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These days most dark matter hunters are trying to hypothesize or detect exotic new particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36260,
    "dDurationMs": 5139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to explain the stuff - and we recently discussed some of the possibilities for brand new particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41399,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics that might explain dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44239,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Others are trying to find holes in Einstein’s\ntheory of gravity - the general theory of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48519,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity - that might explain dark matter’s\ninfluence without the need for actual matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53219,
    "dDurationMs": 5241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is one explanation that may require no new physics whatsoever - an explanation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58460,
    "dDurationMs": 4739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is so bafflingly simple it almost feels\noverlooked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63199,
    "dDurationMs": 3341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if dark matter is just black holes?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66540,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These hyper-dense holes in the fabric of spacetime seem to be great dark matter candidates - being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71680,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so black and holey and all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73720,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we’ve discussed many times before, black holes are regions of gravitational field so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77720,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intense that not even light can escape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81020,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Evidence for the reality of black holes is\nnow pretty convincing - and we’ve talked"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84550,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about this evidence before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85950,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fact that we know black holes are actually real seems like a significant point in their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90150,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "favor as an explanation for dark matter - it’s\nmore than we can say for any of the other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94750,
    "dDurationMs": 2690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter particle candidates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97440,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what would it take for this to be true?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99620,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, dark matter makes up roughly 80% of\nthe mass of the universe, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103830,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it’s much more spread out than regular matter - for example, in our galaxy it forms a vast"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108510,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "halo around twice the diameter of the Milky\nWay’s spiral disk, where most of the stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112790,
    "dDurationMs": 1170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are found."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113960,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So for black holes to be dark matter they’d\nneed to be abundant enough to make up all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118330,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this mass, and they’d need to be spread\nout in the same way that dark matter is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122690,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, most of the physical universe needs to be vast swarms of black holes that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127930,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outweigh all the atoms in the universe by a factor of four."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131460,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are the non-negotiables for black-holes-as-dark-matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135540,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The main remaining variable is the mass of\nthe individual black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139650,
    "dDurationMs": 6610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We could get to the required dark matter mass with lots of massive black holes, or ludicrously"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146260,
    "dDurationMs": 2209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many smaller black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148469,
    "dDurationMs": 4401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have, of course, been trying to find evidence for black hole dark matter for some time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152870,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any given study is sensitive to a particular\nrange of black hole masses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157540,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a study doesn’t find enough of black holes in that range, then that mass range is ruled out as a main"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163640,
    "dDurationMs": 1909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contributor to dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165549,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Scientific method 101 - form a hypothesis,\nthen try to prove it wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170489,
    "dDurationMs": 3481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our hypothesis is that dark matter is made\nof black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173970,
    "dDurationMs": 4409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to go through the mass\nspectrum of black holes, and close one window"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178379,
    "dDurationMs": 4931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after another - we’ll see at the end whether\nthere are any gaps left - whether it’s still"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183310,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible for black holes to explain dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186090,
    "dDurationMs": 4399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But before we start eliminating specific black holes masses, let’s rule out an entire class"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190489,
    "dDurationMs": 6101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of black holes. In face we're going to rule out black holes from the only reliable astrophysical source - dead stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196590,
    "dDurationMs": 3289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know black holes form from the remaining\ncores of the most massive stars, after they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199879,
    "dDurationMs": 2051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explode as supernovae."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201930,
    "dDurationMs": 3429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can make a pretty good estimate of the\nmaximum possible number of these black holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205359,
    "dDurationMs": 5841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by estimating the number of stars that formed and died through cosmic history."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211200,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we look into the distance we’re actually looking back in time, so we can literally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214870,
    "dDurationMs": 2619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see star formation happening in the early\nuniverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217489,
    "dDurationMs": 4411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can also see the products of the supernova explosions- not so much the black holes produced in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221900,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those explosions, but the heavy elements forged in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224270,
    "dDurationMs": 2749,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cores of these stars during their death-throes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227019,
    "dDurationMs": 4881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That star formation history and heavy element abundance tells us there haven’t been anywhere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231900,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "near enough supernovae to give us enough black holes to make up all of dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237290,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, if dark matter is produced as stars\ndie, you’d expect its influence to increase"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242640,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243640,
    "dDurationMs": 3879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we know that dark matter has been with\nus from the very beginning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247519,
    "dDurationMs": 6031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The density fluctuations in the cosmic microwave background tell us that the gravity of dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253550,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter was pulling matter together long before the first stars ever formed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258050,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if dark matter is made of black holes then those black holes must have been with us from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262170,
    "dDurationMs": 1710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the beginning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263880,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately for our hypothesis, there is a reason to think that colossal numbers of black holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269690,
    "dDurationMs": 2630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may have formed in the very early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272320,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call these primordial black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275160,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, we’ve talked about them before, but let’s dig much deeper into the question of whether "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280690,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "primordial black holes could explain dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284530,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are a few ways primordial black holes\ncould form."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288130,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the most mainstream mechanism is that they collapsed from density fluctuations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293260,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know there were regions of the early universe that had a bit more matter than other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297420,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regions - we see that in the cosmic microwave background from around 300,000 years after"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302560,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303700,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those fluctuations would have been much stronger at earlier times and perhaps strong enough that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309480,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most massive of them would have collapsed into black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313030,
    "dDurationMs": 6790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now those black holes should have all formed at around the same mass - but that mass depends"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319820,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the details of the state of the early universe, and could be anything from a grain of salt"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325640,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to tens of thousands of suns."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328160,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because of this, to really falsify the primordial black hole as dark matter hypothesis, we need"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333960,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to rule out this entire mass range."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336180,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, let’s get started."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337730,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll begin with the extreme ends of the mass spectrum - those are easy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343160,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most massive black holes in the universe weigh in at millions to billions of times"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347280,
    "dDurationMs": 1710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mass of our Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348990,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see these “supermassive black holes”\nin the centers of essentially all galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353460,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark matter can’t be made of these or anything close to because they tend to fall to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358910,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "centers of their galaxies pretty quickly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360820,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the opposite end of the mass spectrum we have the black holes under a billion tons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364670,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or around the mass of a small mountain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366750,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can rule these out because by now all of\nthese would have evaporated as Hawking radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372590,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, there’s a terrifying caveat here that\nI’ll save for the very end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377040,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can also rule out black holes a bit larger\nthan this as dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380300,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For dark matter to be made of black holes\nwith masses around that of a larger asteroid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384760,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or small moon, we’d need truly ridiculous\nnumbers of them to get all the dark matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389980,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390980,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m talking somewhere between a billion\nto a billion billion times more of them than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395780,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are stars in the universes.."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398280,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even though these black holes would have microscopic event horizons, at those insane abundances,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403980,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they’d be wreaking absolute havoc in various astrophysical objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408300,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, they’d puncture white dwarfs\nand neutron stars on a regular basis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413200,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A primordial micro-black hole would pass right through a white dwarf, but it would deposit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417990,
    "dDurationMs": 6450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough heat to allow the stars' ultra dense carbon and oxygen to undergo nuclear fusion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424440,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like striking a match, this would ignite a\ncataclysmic chain reaction, exploding the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429630,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star as a type 1a supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432710,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neutron stars are a bit different."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434450,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They're so dense, that they would stop\na microscopic black hole in its tracks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438960,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The black hole would then swallow the neutron star from the inside out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442950,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we see too few type 1a supernova and too many neutron stars for this to be a common"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448980,
    "dDurationMs": 1540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phenomenon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450520,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Probably no more than a few percent of the\ndark matter mass can be from these micro black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455740,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456740,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, let’s move up the black hole mass spectrum to masses around that of a planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460120,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this point, gravitational lensing becomes\nthe go-to method for dark matter hunters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When a compact mass like a black hole passes\nin front of a distant light source, the warped"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470720,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime around the black hole acts like a lens, magnifying the source in an effect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475810,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called microlensing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478610,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Microlensing doesn’t necessarily tell you\nwhether the lensing object is a black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482560,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or some other dark compact mass like a brown dwarf star, neutron star, Dyson sphere or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488580,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a cluster of Reaper capital ships - as long\nas they’re massive, compact, and dwell in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493950,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vast numbers throughout the halo of the Milky Way, watching and waiting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498180,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Broadly we call this breed of dark matter\ncandidate “MACHOs” - massive, compact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503840,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "halo objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504980,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The best way to search for MACHOs in our galaxy is to monitor the stars in the galactic bulge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510530,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or in our neighboring galaxies to see if they\nfluctuate in brightness due to microlensing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515750,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A dedicated telescope on Mt Stromlo in Australia pioneered this work throughout the nineties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521800,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The very first MACHO microlensing events were detected, but there weren’t nearly enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525880,
    "dDurationMs": 1730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to account for dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527610,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since then, further surveys have really nailed down the constraints."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532410,
    "dDurationMs": 4549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Magellanic Clouds are especially good\nfor this - the low level of microlensing of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536959,
    "dDurationMs": 5511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars in these mini-galaxies has allowed us\nto rule out MACHOs between roughly the moon’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542470,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass to 10 or so times the mass of the Sun\nas a main contributor to dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547970,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even if MACHOs aren’t all of dark matter, studies of the Magellanic Clouds and Andromeda"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553100,
    "dDurationMs": 5169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have found enough microlensing events to suggest that 20% of the mass of the Milky Way halo"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558269,
    "dDurationMs": 3791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may be dark, compact objects with the masses of a small star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562060,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we can’t rule out these as primordial black holes, nor as Reapers - but there’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567640,
    "dDurationMs": 2859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no good evidence of either."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570499,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So far we’ve mostly ruled out black holes around the Sun’s mass or lower as an explanation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575819,
    "dDurationMs": 1631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577450,
    "dDurationMs": 4499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Larger black holes are tricky, because you\nneed fewer of them to make up the mass of dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581949,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter - which means they're less likely to spotted through microlensing studies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586899,
    "dDurationMs": 4831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As I mentioned, the most massive black holes trickle to the center of our galaxy, but there’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591730,
    "dDurationMs": 4859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an intermediate mass range from tens to thousands of solar masses that could still be abundant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596589,
    "dDurationMs": 1521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "throughout the Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598110,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One way to test that is to look at dwarf galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601300,
    "dDurationMs": 4149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These galaxies are so small and dense that even black holes with tens of solar masses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605449,
    "dDurationMs": 3971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should have trickled to the center by now,\nand in the process flung less massive stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609420,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into higher orbits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611480,
    "dDurationMs": 4539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Studies of the structures of dwarf galaxies\ntells us that no more than 4% of the dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616019,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter could be black holes of tens to thousands of solar masses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620589,
    "dDurationMs": 5401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The existence of loosely bound binary star\nsystems throughout our galaxy gives us similar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625990,
    "dDurationMs": 5199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constraints - if there were lots of black\nholes of several tens times the Sun’s mass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631189,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then these binaries would long ago have been torn apart by close encounters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635149,
    "dDurationMs": 1961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, so there we have it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637110,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have some evidence ruling out most of the\nblack hole mass spectrum as the main source"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642160,
    "dDurationMs": 1429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643589,
    "dDurationMs": 4961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are a few narrow windows remaining,\nbut these are just the windows where our studies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648550,
    "dDurationMs": 6449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are less sensitive, and frankly it's unlikely that it just so happens that all dark matter is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654999,
    "dDurationMs": 2991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "packed into the spots we haven’t properly looked at yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657990,
    "dDurationMs": 5269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So dark matter probably isn’t black holes\n- but don’t be sad - that means dark matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663259,
    "dDurationMs": 4791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is probably something that physics hasn’t\nexplained yet, which means when we figure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668050,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it out we’ll have a window into NEW physics,\nand who knows what we’ll find there?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673600,
    "dDurationMs": 2510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But speaking of new physics - one last option."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676110,
    "dDurationMs": 6459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s speculated that an evaporated black\nhole might leave behind a tiny naked singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682569,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- a speck of infinite density weighing less\nthan a grain of salt."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687029,
    "dDurationMs": 6691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These so-called Planck relics are bad news\nas dark matter - because they’re essentially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693720,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "undetectable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694720,
    "dDurationMs": 4849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And also because there would have to be a\nfew in the room with your right now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699569,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whatever the case, dark matter is freaky stuff, fitting as the main material ingredient of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705399,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our generally freaky space time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
